import { EffectSequencingCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> EffectSequencingCopy = EffectSequencingCopy {
  summary: "Process items one at a time, choosing to continue, stop successfully, or fail.",
  taskTitle: "Stop at stop, and report bad as a failure",
  purpose: "Print labels in order, finish normally at stop, and reject bad as an invalid label without processing later items. An empty array finishes successfully without visiting anything.",
  typescriptBefore: "TypeScript can use an ordinary for...of loop: break at stop, or print the rejection and return at bad. These examples only print, so no unnecessary asynchronous code is added.",
  benefit: "With forEachUntil, visit returns the decision for one item. Each operation completes in order, with successful stopping kept separate from expected failure.",
  setup: "The std/effect import supplies the effects helpers. visit is the callback for one String. The labels: Array<String> parameter in preview accepts an array of strings.",
  after: [
    "outputFailure is an ordinary function converting an output failure, ConsoleError, into the String output failed. effects.mapError wraps println to give visit a common String failure type. It does not make output infallible.",
    "The do block in effect fn visit first runs println. _ <- waits for completion and discards the result. It then chooses effects.fail for bad, Break for stop, or Continue otherwise. Those control choices are ordinary values, wrapped with effects.succeed to return an operation.",
    "effects.forEachUntil visit labels calls visit for each item and waits for its operation to finish. Continue permits the next item. Break ends successfully without pulling another item. Asynchronous callbacks follow the same sequential rule; failure also prevents later visits.",
    "The traversal succeeds with Unit, a completion-only value, not an array of per-item results. effects.attempt returns Either<String, Unit>: Right for success, or Left for an expected String failure. The value in Right is that Unit.",
    "describe uses match to distinguish Right and Left and return Completed or Rejected: followed by the reason. Its -> String annotation names the return type. println displays the string this ordinary function produces.",
    "In preview, result <- runs attempt and receives the Either. In main, _ <- preview discards the completed value, not a failure. The last preview is main's final operation. Printing the description is itself outside attempt.",
    "The five arrays cover a middle stop, exhaustion, empty input, an immediate stop, and a bad-label failure. None visits the label after. Iterable in the declaration means an input whose items can be obtained one after another; the concrete input here is an array."
  ],
  details: [
    "stop and bad themselves are printed because visit outputs before deciding. Break does not undo the current item. To omit the stopping label from output, make that decision before printing.",
    "Returning a bare Break or Continue does not return the required operation; wrap it with effects.succeed. The traversal's successful value is also not a collected array. Choose a collecting operation if you need per-item results.",
    "If output fails, execution stops before the label decision and Left receives output failed. Break means success; effects.fail remains failure. Runtime defects and cancellation are not expected failures that attempt turns into Left."
  ]
}
